      title: "The Idea of a Renaissance",
      body: `## A word and a claim

The word **Renaissance**—French for "rebirth"—was popularized by nineteenth-century historians, but the idea is much older. It was invented by the people who believed they were living through it. In the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, Italian writers and artists came to believe that the arts and learning of ancient Greece and Rome had been lost for a thousand years, buried under what they called the darkness of the "middle age," and that they were bringing them back to life.

The poet **Petrarch** (1304–1374), the first great figure of the movement, spoke of the centuries since the fall of Rome as a time of "darkness," and hoped that a better age might follow: "Perhaps, when the darkness has been dispersed, our descendants will be able to return to the pure radiance of the past." A century later, Florentine writers were confident that the better age had come. The philosopher **Marsilio Ficino** wrote in 1492: "This century, like a golden age, has restored to light the liberal arts, which were almost extinct: grammar, poetry, rhetoric, painting, sculpture, architecture, music."

The painter and architect **Giorgio Vasari**, in his ***Lives of the Most Excellent Painters, Sculptors, and Architects*** (1550), gave this idea its classic form. Art, he said, had flourished in antiquity, died with the barbarian invasions, and been reborn—*rinascita*—in Tuscany, beginning with the painter Giotto around 1300, and rising through three ages to the perfection of Michelangelo. Vasari's book is the founding work of art history, and its story of decline and rebirth shaped the way Europeans thought about their past for centuries.

## Burckhardt

The modern idea of the Renaissance as a distinct period of European history was created by the Swiss historian **Jacob Burckhardt**, in ***The Civilization of the Renaissance in Italy*** (1860), one of the most influential history books ever written. Burckhardt argued that in Renaissance Italy, for the first time, the modern European spirit was born.

In the Middle Ages, Burckhardt wrote, human consciousness "lay dreaming or half awake beneath a common veil," woven of "faith, illusion, and childish prepossession." Man was conscious of himself "only as a member of a race, people, party, family, or corporation." In Italy, "this veil first melted into air"; "man became a spiritual *individual*, and recognized himself as such." Burckhardt traced the new spirit through every aspect of Italian life: the state as "a work of art," calculated and organized; the development of the individual, with his ambition for fame; the revival of antiquity; the "discovery of the world and of man"—of landscape, of the human body, of biography and autobiography; and a new, worldly morality, often unscrupulous, sometimes pagan.

## The revolt of the medievalists

Burckhardt's picture was enormously persuasive, and also, as later historians showed, exaggerated. In the twentieth century, historians of the Middle Ages mounted what one scholar called "the revolt of the medievalists." They pointed out that the Middle Ages had known several "renaissances" of classical learning: the Carolingian Renaissance of the ninth century, under Charlemagne, which preserved most of the Latin classics we have; and the "**Renaissance of the twelfth century**," as the American historian **Charles Homer Haskins** called it in 1927, which saw the founding of the universities, the recovery of Aristotle, and the revival of Roman law. Medieval people had not been unconscious of themselves as individuals. And many features of "Renaissance" culture—its piety, its belief in astrology and magic, its social hierarchies—were thoroughly medieval.

Other historians questioned whether the Renaissance mattered much to most people. The great majority of Europeans in 1500 were peasants who could not read, whose lives were little changed by humanism or perspective painting. The Renaissance, in this view, was the culture of a small elite: scholars, artists, merchants, and princes.

## What remains

Most historians today use "the Renaissance" more modestly. They mean a cultural movement, centered in Italy between about 1350 and 1550, and spreading to the rest of Europe in the sixteenth century, that sought to revive the literature, learning, and art of classical antiquity, and in doing so produced something new. It was not a sudden break with the Middle Ages, nor the birth of the modern world. But it was real: the people who lived through it believed they were doing something new, and the art and thought they produced changed European culture permanently.

Its central intellectual movement was **humanism**: the study of the classical languages and literature, of grammar, rhetoric, poetry, history, and moral philosophy—the *studia humanitatis*, or "humanities." Its central artistic achievement was the development of a new, naturalistic art based on the study of ancient sculpture, the human body, and the laws of perspective. And its home, for its first two centuries, was the cities of northern and central Italy. In this lesson, we follow it there; in the next, we follow it north of the Alps, where it met the printing press and the reform of the church, and helped to set in motion the Reformation.

## Why Italy?

Why did the Renaissance begin in Italy? Several answers have been proposed. Italy was the most urban and commercial region of Europe, with great cities—Florence, Venice, Milan, Genoa—whose merchants and bankers had the wealth to support art and learning. Its cities were independent, competitive, and proud, eager to glorify themselves. The ruins of ancient Rome were everywhere, a constant reminder of a vanished greatness that Italians regarded as their own heritage. Italian education emphasized Latin rhetoric and law, the training of notaries and lawyers, which led naturally to the study of classical texts. And Italy had close contact with the Byzantine world, where Greek learning had been preserved.

> **Pause and consider.** Burckhardt said that in Renaissance Italy "man became a spiritual individual." What would count as evidence for or against such a claim?`,
