      title: "The Italian City-States",
      body: `## A land of cities

Italy in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries was not a nation. It was a patchwork of independent states, large and small, often at war with one another. In the south lay the **Kingdom of Naples**, ruled by French and then Spanish dynasties. In the center lay the **Papal States**, ruled by the pope from Rome, though the popes lived in Avignon, in France, from 1309 to 1377, and their authority over the region was often nominal. In the north lay the great city-states: the **Duchy of Milan**, ruled by the Visconti and then the Sforza dukes; the **Republic of Venice**; and the **Republic of Florence**, along with many smaller states such as Mantua, Ferrara, Urbino, Siena, and Genoa.

These states had grown out of the medieval **communes**, self-governing cities that had won their independence from the Holy Roman Emperor and local lords in the eleventh and twelfth centuries. By the fourteenth century, many had fallen under the rule of single lords, called **signori**, who often began as military captains or leaders of factions and founded hereditary dynasties. Others, like Venice and Florence, remained republics, governed by councils of wealthy citizens.

## Venice

**Venice**, built on islands in a lagoon at the head of the Adriatic, was the greatest commercial power in the Mediterranean. Its merchants traded with Constantinople, Alexandria, and the ports of the Levant, bringing spices, silk, and cotton to Europe. Its arsenal, the largest industrial enterprise in Europe, could build a galley in a day. Venice was governed by a closed aristocracy of merchant families, whose names were inscribed in a "Golden Book," and who elected the **doge**, the head of state, for life, through an elaborate process designed to prevent any one family from gaining power. Its stability, in a land of revolutions and tyrants, made Venice famous as "the most serene republic."

## Florence

**Florence**, on the Arno River in Tuscany, was the center of the early Renaissance. With perhaps a hundred thousand inhabitants before the Black Death of 1348, which killed perhaps half of them, it was one of the largest cities in Europe. Its wealth came from the manufacture of fine woolen cloth and from banking: Florentine bankers lent money to the pope, to the kings of England and France, and to merchants across Europe, and the Florentine gold coin, the **florin**, was the standard currency of European trade.

Florence was a republic, at least in name. Its government was in the hands of the **guilds**, the associations of merchants and craftsmen, and especially of the seven "major guilds" of bankers, cloth merchants, lawyers, and the like. Offices were filled by lot, for short terms, from lists of eligible citizens; the city's chief magistrates, the Signoria, served for only two months. In practice, a small number of wealthy families dominated the city. In 1378, the wool workers, the **Ciompi**, who had no guild of their own and no political rights, rose in revolt and briefly seized the government, before being crushed.

## The Medici

In the fifteenth century, Florence came under the control of one family: the **Medici**. Their power rested on the Medici bank, the largest in Europe, with branches in Rome, Venice, Geneva, Bruges, and London, and on its role as the pope's banker.

**Cosimo de' Medici** (1389–1464) returned from a brief exile in 1434 and dominated Florence for thirty years without holding any formal office. He ruled through allies, clients, and the manipulation of elections, while preserving the forms of the republic. He was also one of the greatest patrons in history. He paid for the rebuilding of churches and monasteries, commissioned works from the sculptor Donatello and the architect Michelozzo, built a great library at the monastery of San Marco, and supported the scholar Marsilio Ficino's translation of Plato. A pious man, he is said to have worried about the sin of usury, and to have spent lavishly on churches partly to atone for it. After his death, the city voted him the title *Pater Patriae*, "Father of His Country."

His grandson, **Lorenzo de' Medici** (1449–1492), called **Lorenzo the Magnificent**, was a poet, a patron of artists and scholars, and a skillful diplomat who kept the peace among the Italian states. In 1478, the rival Pazzi family, with the support of Pope Sixtus IV, tried to assassinate Lorenzo and his brother Giuliano during Mass in the cathedral; Giuliano was killed, Lorenzo escaped, and the conspirators were hanged from the windows of the government palace. Under Lorenzo, the Medici court was the center of Florentine culture: the young Michelangelo lived in his household, and the painter Botticelli worked for his family.

## Patronage

The art of the Renaissance was paid for by **patrons**: guilds, churches, confraternities, governments, and wealthy families. A patron typically signed a contract with an artist, specifying the subject, the materials, the size, the price, and sometimes the amount of expensive blue pigment (made from lapis lazuli) to be used. Patrons commissioned art for many reasons: to honor God and the saints, to atone for sins, to display wealth and taste, to glorify a family or a city, to win the favor of the people. The great public works of Florence—the cathedral dome, the bronze doors of the Baptistery, the statues on the guild church of Orsanmichele—were commissioned by guilds and civic bodies, in competition with one another and with rival cities.

## Princes and courts

The courts of the princely states were also great centers of culture. In **Urbino**, Duke **Federico da Montefeltro** (1422–1482), a professional soldier who earned a fortune as a mercenary captain, built a magnificent palace and a library of Greek and Latin manuscripts, and was painted in profile by Piero della Francesca. In **Mantua**, the Gonzaga family employed the painter Andrea Mantegna. In **Ferrara**, the Este dukes supported the poet Ariosto. In **Milan**, Duke Ludovico Sforza employed **Leonardo da Vinci** as painter, engineer, and designer of court festivals. The courts were the setting for Baldassare Castiglione's ***The Book of the Courtier*** (1528), which described the ideal courtier: skilled in arms, letters, music, and conversation, and able to do everything with an effortless grace he called *sprezzatura*.

> **Pause and consider.** Cosimo de' Medici ruled Florence for thirty years while preserving the forms of a republic. Was Florence still a republic? What makes a government republican?`,
