      title: "Petrarch and the Humanists",
      body: `## The father of humanism

**Francesco Petrarca**, known in English as **Petrarch**, was born in 1304 in Arezzo, the son of a Florentine notary who had been exiled from Florence (in the same political purge that exiled Dante). He grew up in Avignon, where the popes then lived, studied law at his father's insistence, and abandoned it after his father's death for literature. He became the most famous writer in Europe: in 1341, he was crowned poet laureate on the Capitoline Hill in Rome, in a ceremony he believed revived an ancient Roman custom.

Petrarch is best remembered today for his Italian love poems, the ***Canzoniere***, addressed to a woman he called **Laura**, whom he first saw in a church in Avignon on Good Friday, 1327, and loved, without return, for the rest of his life. His sonnets were imitated across Europe for three centuries and shaped the love poetry of Shakespeare and his contemporaries. But Petrarch himself valued above all his Latin works, and his lifelong effort to recover and imitate the writers of ancient Rome.

## The love of antiquity

Petrarch searched the monastic libraries of Europe for lost classical texts. In 1333, in Liège, he found two lost speeches of Cicero; in 1345, in the cathedral library of Verona, he found a manuscript of Cicero's letters to his friend Atticus, which revealed a Cicero very different from the solemn philosopher of the schools: anxious, ambitious, gossiping, sometimes petty. Petrarch was so moved that he wrote a letter to Cicero himself, addressed to the dead man "in the land of the living," reproaching him for his involvement in politics. He wrote letters to Livy, to Virgil, to Homer. He treated the ancients as friends and contemporaries, more real to him than the people around him.

Petrarch also condemned what he saw as the barbarism of his own age. He attacked the scholastic philosophers of the universities, with their technical Latin and their endless logical disputes about Aristotle, which he thought useless for living a good life. What mattered, he believed, was not abstract knowledge but **wisdom** and **virtue**, and the best guides to these were the moral philosophers, poets, and historians of antiquity, read in their own elegant Latin. "It is better to will the good than to know the truth," he wrote.

## The ascent of Mont Ventoux

One of Petrarch's most famous letters describes his ascent of **Mont Ventoux**, a mountain in Provence, with his brother, in April 1336 (though he may have written or revised the letter much later). He climbed, he says, "simply out of a desire to see its conspicuous height." At the summit, he gazed at the view of the Alps, the Rhône, and the sea. Then he opened a little copy of St. Augustine's ***Confessions*** that he always carried with him, and his eyes fell on this passage: "And men go about to wonder at the heights of the mountains, and the mighty waves of the sea, and the wide sweep of rivers, and the circuit of the ocean, and the revolution of the stars, but themselves they consider not."

Petrarch was struck dumb. He closed the book, "angry with myself that I should still be admiring earthly things," and descended the mountain in silence. The letter has often been read as a symbol of the Renaissance: the first modern man climbing a mountain for the sake of the view. But it is also a deeply Christian, Augustinian text, in which the climb becomes an allegory of the soul's ascent and its turn inward to God. Petrarch was both: a lover of antiquity and a devout Christian, who never resolved the tension between them.

## The humanist movement

After Petrarch, his followers made the study of classical antiquity into a movement. They called their program the ***studia humanitatis***: the study of grammar, rhetoric, poetry, history, and moral philosophy, based on the reading of classical authors. (The word "humanist," *umanista*, was originally student slang for a teacher of these subjects, like *legista* for a teacher of law.) The humanists were not opposed to religion, and they were not "humanists" in the modern sense of people who reject the supernatural. They were scholars, teachers, and writers who believed that the study of classical literature would make men wiser, more eloquent, and more virtuous.

Humanists worked as teachers, secretaries, and diplomats, for popes, princes, and city governments, who valued their skill in writing elegant Latin letters and speeches. They founded schools that educated the sons of the elite—and sometimes the daughters. **Vittorino da Feltre**'s school at Mantua, founded in 1423, taught Latin and Greek, mathematics, music, and physical exercise, and admitted some poor students free of charge. Humanist education became the standard education of the European elite for four hundred years, and is the ancestor of the "liberal arts" education of the modern American college.

## Civic humanism

In Florence, humanism took a political turn. **Coluccio Salutati** (1331–1406), chancellor of the republic for thirty years, used his eloquent Latin letters as weapons of diplomacy in Florence's wars against Milan; the duke of Milan is said to have remarked that a letter of Salutati's was worth a thousand cavalry. His successor, **Leonardo Bruni** (c. 1370–1444), wrote a history of Florence modeled on Livy and a ***Panegyric to the City of Florence*** (c. 1403–1404), in which he praised the city as the heir of the Roman Republic, the defender of liberty against tyranny.

In 1955, the German American historian **Hans Baron** argued, in *The Crisis of the Early Italian Renaissance*, that Florentine humanism was transformed around 1400 by the city's struggle for survival against the expansionist duke of Milan, Giangaleazzo Visconti. In this crisis, Baron argued, humanists like Bruni developed a "**civic humanism**" that celebrated active participation in public life, republican liberty, and the duty of citizens to serve the state, in contrast to the medieval ideal of contemplative withdrawal from the world. Baron's thesis has been much debated: critics have pointed out problems with his dating of texts, and noted that Bruni's praise of liberty was also propaganda for an oligarchy. But the idea of civic humanism has had a lasting influence on historians of political thought, who have traced its ideals of active citizenship and republican virtue through Machiavelli to the English republicans of the seventeenth century and the American founders.

## The humanist hunt for books

The humanists continued Petrarch's search for lost classical texts. The greatest of the book hunters was **Poggio Bracciolini** (1380–1459), a papal secretary who, during the Council of Constance (1414–1418), searched the monastic libraries of Switzerland and Germany. At the monastery of St. Gall, he found a complete text of Quintilian's *Institutes of Oratory*. In 1417, probably at the abbey of Fulda, he found the only surviving copy of **Lucretius**'s *On the Nature of Things*, a poem that set out the materialist philosophy of Epicurus: that the world is made of atoms moving in a void, that the gods do not intervene in human affairs, and that the soul dies with the body. The American scholar **Stephen Greenblatt**, in *The Swerve* (2011), argued that Poggio's discovery helped make the modern world; many Renaissance historians think he exaggerated.

> **Pause and consider.** Petrarch climbed a mountain to see the view, then turned inward with Augustine. Is his letter a symbol of the new age or of the old?`,
