      title: "Recovering the Greeks",
      body: `## Greek in the West

For most of the Middle Ages, almost no one in Western Europe could read Greek. The works of Plato, Homer, the Greek tragedians, and the Greek historians were known, if at all, only through Latin summaries and references. Aristotle had been recovered in Latin translation, much of it from Arabic, in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries. Petrarch owned a manuscript of Homer, which he kissed but could not read.

That changed at the end of the fourteenth century. In **1397**, the Florentine government invited the Byzantine scholar **Manuel Chrysoloras** to teach Greek in Florence. He stayed only three years, but he taught a generation of humanists, including Leonardo Bruni, who went on to translate Plato, Aristotle, Plutarch, and Demosthenes into Latin. Chrysoloras's Greek grammar became the standard textbook. Florentine and Venetian scholars traveled to Constantinople to learn Greek and buy manuscripts.

## The Council of Florence

In **1438–1439**, the Byzantine emperor John VIII Palaeologus, desperate for Western help against the Ottoman Turks, came to Italy with the patriarch of Constantinople and a delegation of hundreds of Greek churchmen and scholars to negotiate the reunion of the Greek and Latin churches at the **Council of Ferrara–Florence**. The union of the churches, proclaimed in 1439, was rejected by the people of Constantinople and soon collapsed. But the council had another, unexpected consequence. Among the Greek delegates was the aged philosopher **George Gemistos Plethon**, a passionate Platonist, who lectured to the Florentines on Plato. According to Ficino, Cosimo de' Medici was so inspired that he resolved to revive the Platonic Academy in Florence.

Another delegate, **Basilios Bessarion**, archbishop of Nicaea, accepted the union, became a cardinal of the Roman church, and settled in Italy. He collected more than a thousand Greek manuscripts, which he gave to Venice in 1468; they became the foundation of the great Marciana Library.

## The fall of Constantinople

On May 29, **1453**, Constantinople fell to the Ottoman sultan **Mehmed II**, after a siege of fifty-three days. The last Byzantine emperor, Constantine XI, died fighting on the walls. The city's great church, Hagia Sophia, became a mosque. The fall of Constantinople shocked Europe, and it accelerated the flight of Greek scholars and manuscripts to Italy. The idea, once common, that the fall of Constantinople "caused" the Renaissance is wrong—the study of Greek in Italy was well under way by 1453—but it did bring many Greek teachers and books to the West.

## Ficino and Plato

**Marsilio Ficino** (1433–1499), the son of Cosimo de' Medici's doctor, was raised in the Medici household and trained in Greek. Cosimo gave him a villa at Careggi, outside Florence, and commissioned him to translate the complete works of **Plato** into Latin, which he completed in 1468, and published in 1484. It was the first complete translation of Plato into any Western language, and it remained the standard for centuries. Ficino also translated the works of **Plotinus** and the other Neoplatonists, and the ***Corpus Hermeticum***, a collection of mystical texts attributed to an ancient Egyptian sage, **Hermes Trismegistus**, which Ficino believed to be older than Moses—Cosimo, near death, asked him to translate it before Plato. (In 1614, the scholar Isaac Casaubon showed that the Hermetic texts were in fact written in the early Christian centuries.)

Ficino was a priest and believed that Platonism was compatible with Christianity: that Plato had glimpsed, by reason, the truths that Christ had revealed. He developed a Christian Platonism that emphasized the immortality of the soul, the ascent of the mind to God through the love of beauty, and the dignity of the human soul as the link between the material and spiritual worlds. He coined the phrase "**Platonic love**," meaning a spiritual love between friends that leads the soul toward God. His group of friends and disciples, which met at Careggi, became known as the **Platonic Academy**, though it was less a formal institution than a circle of conversation.

## Pico and the dignity of man

Ficino's most brilliant follower was **Giovanni Pico della Mirandola** (1463–1494), a young count of prodigious learning, who knew Latin, Greek, Hebrew, Arabic, and Aramaic, and studied the Jewish mystical tradition of the **Kabbalah**. In 1486, at the age of twenty-three, he proposed to defend 900 theses on every subject in philosophy and theology at a public disputation in Rome, and invited scholars from all over Europe to attend at his expense. He wrote an introductory speech, later known as the ***Oration on the Dignity of Man***, which became one of the most famous texts of the Renaissance.

In the *Oration*, Pico imagines God, at the end of the Creation, wishing for a being who could contemplate and admire his work. All the places in the universe had been filled, so God made man a creature of no fixed nature, and placed him at the center of the world, saying: "We have given you, Adam, no fixed seat, no form of your own, no gift peculiar to you alone. … You, constrained by no limits, in accordance with your own free will, in whose hand we have placed you, shall ordain for yourself the limits of your nature. … You may degenerate into the lower forms of life, which are brutish; you may, by the judgment of your own soul, be reborn into the higher forms, which are divine."

The passage has often been read as a manifesto of Renaissance individualism and human freedom. In context, it is a Christian and Platonic argument: man's freedom is the freedom to ascend toward God through philosophy and contemplation. The pope found thirteen of the theses suspicious of heresy, and the disputation never took place. Pico fled to France, was briefly imprisoned, and returned to Florence under Lorenzo's protection. He died at thirty-one, possibly poisoned.

## Valla and the Donation of Constantine

The humanists' study of language also gave them a powerful critical tool. **Lorenzo Valla** (1407–1457), a brilliant and combative scholar, argued that language changes over time, and that by studying the Latin of a text, one could determine when it was written. In **1440**, while working for the king of Naples, who was at war with the pope, Valla applied this method to the **Donation of Constantine**, a document in which the emperor Constantine supposedly gave the pope authority over Rome and the western half of the empire. The popes had used it for centuries to justify their temporal power.

Valla showed that the Donation's Latin contained words and expressions that did not exist in the fourth century, and anachronisms that no contemporary of Constantine could have written. It was, he proved, a forgery, probably written in the eighth century. His treatise was not published until 1517, when German reformers printed it as ammunition against the papacy. Valla also compared the Latin Vulgate Bible with the Greek New Testament and found errors in the translation—work that would inspire Erasmus. His method, the historical criticism of texts, is one of the foundations of modern scholarship.

> **Pause and consider.** Pico's God tells Adam that he may make himself whatever he chooses. Is this a Christian vision of human nature or a challenge to one?`,
