      title: "The New Art",
      body: `## Brunelleschi's dome

In **1418**, the wool guild of Florence, which was responsible for building the city's cathedral, held a competition. The cathedral had been under construction since 1296, and its nave was finished, but it lacked a dome over the enormous octagonal crossing, more than 140 feet across—wider than any dome built since the Pantheon in ancient Rome. No one knew how to build it. The usual method, building a wooden framework (centering) to support the masonry while it was laid, was impossible at that size: there was not enough timber in Tuscany.

The competition was won by **Filippo Brunelleschi** (1377–1446), a goldsmith who had studied the ruins of ancient Rome. His solution was ingenious: a double-shelled dome, built without centering, using bricks laid in a herringbone pattern so that each ring supported itself as it rose, and rings of stone and chain to hold it together. He invented new machines to lift the materials, including a hoist driven by oxen with a reversible gear. The dome was completed in **1436**, and consecrated by Pope Eugenius IV. It still dominates the skyline of Florence, and it remains the largest masonry dome in the world. The architect **Leon Battista Alberti** called it "a structure so great, rising above the skies, ample to cover with its shadow all the Tuscan people."

## Perspective

Brunelleschi is also credited, by his early biographer, with the discovery of **linear perspective**: a mathematical method for representing three-dimensional space on a flat surface, in which parallel lines converge at a single vanishing point on the horizon. Around 1415, he painted two small panels of Florentine buildings and showed them to viewers through a peephole, with a mirror, so that the painting could be compared directly with the real scene. Alberti codified the method in his treatise ***On Painting*** (1435), the first theoretical work on painting since antiquity, which he dedicated to Brunelleschi. A painting, Alberti wrote, should be like "an open window" through which the viewer sees the scene.

## Masaccio, Donatello, Ghiberti

The new methods transformed Florentine art in a single generation. The young painter **Masaccio** (1401–c. 1428), who died at twenty-six, used perspective, light, and solid, weighty figures to create an illusion of real space and real bodies. His fresco of the ***Holy Trinity*** (c. 1427) in the church of Santa Maria Novella shows the Trinity in a barrel-vaulted chapel so convincingly painted that it seems to open a hole in the wall. His frescoes in the **Brancacci Chapel**, showing Adam and Eve expelled from Paradise, weeping, and St. Peter's life, were studied by every later Florentine painter, including Michelangelo.

The sculptor **Donatello** (c. 1386–1466) revived ancient forms and gave them new emotional power. His bronze ***David*** (probably 1440s), commissioned for the Medici palace, was the first freestanding nude statue since antiquity: a slender, sensual adolescent in a shepherd's hat, standing on the head of Goliath. **Lorenzo Ghiberti** spent fifty years making two sets of bronze doors for the Baptistery of Florence; the second set, completed in 1452, with ten gilded panels of Old Testament scenes in deep perspective, was later called by Michelangelo "worthy to be the Gates of Paradise," and is still known by that name.

## The study of nature

Renaissance artists studied nature with new intensity. They drew from life, studied anatomy (some dissected corpses), observed the effects of light and atmosphere, and learned the proportions of the human body from ancient statues and from geometry. Alberti argued that the painter should be educated in geometry, history, and poetry, and should be the companion of scholars and poets—a claim that the painter was not a mere craftsman but a learned man, practicing a liberal art.

This was a new idea of the artist, and it changed the artist's social position. Medieval artists were anonymous craftsmen, members of guilds. By the sixteenth century, the greatest artists were celebrities, courted by popes and kings, and their works were valued as expressions of individual genius. Vasari's *Lives* is a book about individual artists, their personalities, and their achievements.

## The art and its purposes

It is important not to read Renaissance art as secular. The great majority of Renaissance paintings and sculptures were religious: altarpieces, frescoes of the lives of Christ, the Virgin, and the saints, devotional images for the home. The new naturalism served devotion: by making the holy figures seem real, present, and human, it moved the viewer to pity, love, and prayer. Masaccio's weeping Adam and Eve, Donatello's emaciated wooden *Mary Magdalene*, and the tender Madonnas of Fra Filippo Lippi and Raphael were designed to make the sacred story vivid.

But Renaissance art also took up classical subjects, especially in the private palaces and villas of the elite. **Sandro Botticelli** (c. 1445–1510), who worked for the Medici, painted the ***Birth of Venus*** and ***Primavera*** in the 1480s, mythological scenes whose meanings, perhaps shaped by Ficino's Neoplatonism, have been endlessly debated. Botticelli later fell under the influence of the friar Savonarola, and, according to Vasari, burned some of his own secular paintings.

## Leonardo

**Leonardo da Vinci** (1452–1519) was the son of a notary and a peasant woman, born in the village of Vinci near Florence. Trained in the workshop of the Florentine painter and sculptor Verrocchio, he became the most famous painter of his age, though he completed very few paintings. His ***Last Supper*** (c. 1495–1498), painted on a wall of a monastery refectory in Milan, captures the moment when Christ announces that one of his disciples will betray him, and each disciple reacts with a different gesture of shock, grief, or denial. His ***Mona Lisa*** (begun c. 1503), with its soft modeling of light and shadow (*sfumato*) and its enigmatic smile, became the most famous painting in the world.

Leonardo filled thousands of notebook pages, written in mirror script, with observations and drawings: of anatomy, based on his own dissections; of water, flight, botany, geology, and optics; and of machines, real and imaginary—flying machines, armored vehicles, canals, and weapons. He represents for many the ideal of the "Renaissance man," curious about everything. But most of his scientific work was never published, and had little influence on the development of science.

> **Pause and consider.** Alberti said a painting should be "an open window." What does that idea assume about what a painting is for?`,
