      body: `## Savonarola

The golden age of Medici Florence ended in the 1490s. After Lorenzo's death in 1492, his son Piero proved incompetent. In 1494, when the French king **Charles VIII** invaded Italy with a large army, Piero surrendered Florentine fortresses to him without a fight, and the Florentines drove the Medici out of the city.

Into the vacuum stepped **Girolamo Savonarola** (1452–1498), a Dominican friar from Ferrara, prior of the convent of San Marco, whose apocalyptic sermons had been drawing enormous crowds. Savonarola had prophesied that God would send a scourge to punish Italy for its sins, and the French invasion seemed to fulfill his prophecy. He denounced the corruption of the church, the luxury of the rich, and the paganism of humanist culture. Under his influence, the restored Florentine republic set up a broad Great Council and tried to make Florence a holy city. During Carnival in 1497 and 1498, his followers held "**bonfires of the vanities**," burning mirrors, cosmetics, fine clothes, playing cards, musical instruments, books of poetry, and paintings.

Savonarola's attacks on the corrupt **Pope Alexander VI** (Rodrigo Borgia) led to his excommunication. Florentine opinion turned against him. In 1498, he was arrested, tortured, and burned at the stake in the main square of Florence, the Piazza della Signoria, where his bonfires had burned. Michelangelo, who had heard him preach, is said to have remembered the sound of his voice all his life.

## Michelangelo

**Michelangelo Buonarroti** (1475–1564) was the greatest artist of the Renaissance, and perhaps the first artist to be called "divine" in his lifetime. Raised in the household of Lorenzo de' Medici, he was a sculptor above all, who believed that the figure lay hidden in the block of marble, and that the sculptor's task was to free it by cutting away the excess stone.

In 1501, the Florentine republic gave him a huge block of marble, which another sculptor had abandoned decades before, and commissioned a statue of **David**. Michelangelo worked on it for three years. The result, completed in **1504**, a colossal nude youth, seventeen feet tall, standing alert before his fight with Goliath, was placed outside the government palace as a symbol of the republic, which had defeated its enemies by courage and virtue—and of its defiance of the exiled Medici.

## The Sistine Chapel

In 1508, Pope **Julius II** (1503–1513), the "warrior pope," who personally led armies to reconquer the Papal States, summoned Michelangelo to Rome to paint the ceiling of the **Sistine Chapel**, the pope's private chapel in the Vatican. Michelangelo protested that he was a sculptor, not a painter. He spent four years, from 1508 to 1512, on scaffolding sixty feet above the floor, painting more than three hundred figures: scenes from Genesis, from the Creation to the Flood, including the famous ***Creation of Adam***, in which God reaches out to give life to Adam; the prophets and sibyls who foretold the coming of Christ; and the ancestors of Christ. A poem he wrote at the time complains of his neck bent back, his beard pointing to heaven, and the paint dripping on his face.

Twenty-five years later, he returned to paint the ***Last Judgment*** (1536–1541) on the altar wall: a vast, terrifying vision of Christ as judge, with the saved rising and the damned falling into hell. Its hundreds of nude figures provoked outrage after the Council of Trent, and after Michelangelo's death another painter was hired to add draperies.

## Raphael

While Michelangelo was painting the Sistine ceiling, the young painter **Raphael** of Urbino (1483–1520) was decorating the pope's private apartments in the Vatican. His most famous fresco, ***The School of Athens*** (1509–1511), shows the philosophers of ancient Greece gathered in a magnificent classical building: at the center, **Plato**, pointing upward to the realm of ideas, and **Aristotle**, gesturing toward the earth. Raphael gave some of the philosophers the faces of his contemporaries: Plato is said to resemble Leonardo, and the brooding Heraclitus in the foreground may be a portrait of Michelangelo. On the opposite wall, Raphael painted the ***Disputation of the Holy Sacrament***, showing theologians gathered around the Eucharist, with heaven above. Together, the two frescoes express the Renaissance hope of harmonizing classical philosophy and Christian faith.

Raphael's serene Madonnas became the model of ideal beauty for three centuries. He died on Good Friday 1520, at the age of thirty-seven, and was buried in the Pantheon.

## New St. Peter's

In 1506, Julius II laid the foundation stone of a new **St. Peter's Basilica**, to replace the crumbling fourth-century basilica built by Constantine over the traditional site of the apostle Peter's tomb. The architect **Donato Bramante** designed a vast centralized church crowned by a dome inspired by the Pantheon. The project took more than a century, and passed through the hands of many architects, including Raphael and Michelangelo, who designed the great dome, completed after his death. To pay for it, the popes sold **indulgences** across Europe—a practice that, as we will see, provoked a German monk named Martin Luther in 1517.

## The Renaissance papacy

The Renaissance popes were great patrons, but many were also worldly princes, more concerned with politics, war, and their families than with the spiritual life of the church. **Sixtus IV** (1471–1484) built the Sistine Chapel and the Vatican Library, and plotted the murder of the Medici. **Alexander VI** (1492–1503), who had several children, used the papacy to build a principality for his son **Cesare Borgia**. **Julius II** led armies in person. **Leo X** (1513–1521), a son of Lorenzo de' Medici, is said to have remarked, "God has given us the papacy; let us enjoy it." (The remark may be apocryphal, but it captured his reputation.) Their worldliness, and the taxes they levied to pay for their wars and buildings, would provoke resentment across Europe.

## The end of the Italian Renaissance

The High Renaissance in Rome ended with catastrophe. In **1527**, the unpaid troops of the emperor Charles V, many of them German Lutherans, mutinied and sacked Rome, killing thousands, looting churches, and holding the pope prisoner. The **Sack of Rome** shocked Europe. Italy, the center of European culture for two centuries, became a battleground for foreign powers, dominated by Spain. The Renaissance, however, had already spread north, where it would take a different form.

> **Pause and consider.** Raphael painted Plato and Aristotle on one wall and the Eucharist on the other. Did the Renaissance reconcile classical philosophy and Christian faith, or only place them side by side?`,
