      title: "Did Women Have a Renaissance?",
      body: `## A famous question

In 1977, the American historian **Joan Kelly** (who published under the name Joan Kelly-Gadol) published an essay with a provocative title: "**Did Women Have a Renaissance?**" Her answer was no, or at least not during the Renaissance. The period that historians had celebrated as an age of individualism and human freedom had, she argued, actually narrowed the lives of upper-class women.

In the courts of the High Middle Ages, Kelly argued, noblewomen had enjoyed real power and independence: they inherited and managed estates, and in the literature of courtly love, the lady was the sovereign of her lover. In the Renaissance city-states and courts, by contrast, upper-class women were increasingly confined to the household, excluded from public life, and expected to be chaste, silent, and obedient. Castiglione's *Courtier* described an ideal lady whose role was to be ornamental and charming, a support for the courtier's self-display. "There was no renaissance for women," Kelly concluded, "at least, not during the Renaissance."

Kelly's essay launched decades of research on women in the Renaissance, which has produced a more complicated picture. Some women gained, some lost; much depended on class, region, and individual circumstances.

## Women and the family

For most women in Renaissance Italy, life was defined by marriage, the family, and religion. Among the elite, marriages were arranged by families for political and economic advantage. Girls were often married in their mid-teens to men in their late twenties or thirties. The bride's family paid a **dowry**, which grew so expensive in Florence that in 1425 the city created a public fund, the *Monte delle doti*, in which fathers could invest to provide dowries for their daughters. Families that could not afford dowries for all their daughters often placed some in convents: by the sixteenth century, perhaps half the daughters of the Florentine and Venetian patriciate became nuns.

Most women worked: in the household, in the family shop, in the textile industry, as servants, wet nurses, and midwives. Their lives are harder to recover than those of the elite, because they left fewer records. But tax records, court records, and household accounts have allowed historians to reconstruct some of their experience.

## Learned women

A small number of women received a humanist education and became writers and scholars. **Isotta Nogarola** (1418–1466) of Verona, educated in Latin and Greek, corresponded with leading humanists and wrote a famous dialogue on whether Adam or Eve was more to blame for the Fall—she argued, with some irony, that Eve was less guilty because she was weaker. After facing ridicule and slander (an anonymous writer accused her of incest), she withdrew from public life. **Cassandra Fedele** (1465–1558) of Venice gave a Latin oration at the University of Padua and corresponded with Queen Isabella of Spain. **Laura Cereta** (1469–1499) of Brescia wrote letters defending women's capacity for learning, and attacking both men who scorned learned women and women who scorned learning.

Their experience shows both the possibilities and the limits of humanism for women. Humanists praised learned women as marvels, but often as exceptions—"prodigies" who had transcended their sex. A learned woman who sought a public career faced suspicion and hostility. Most were expected to stop writing when they married or entered a convent.

## Christine de Pizan

The most remarkable woman writer of the age lived not in Italy but in France. **Christine de Pizan** (1364–c. 1430) was born in Venice, the daughter of a physician and astrologer who served the French king, and grew up at the French court. Widowed at twenty-five with three children and an aged mother to support, she became the first woman in Europe known to have earned her living as a writer, producing poetry, history, political theory, and books of advice.

In ***The Book of the City of Ladies*** (1405), she imagined three allegorical women—Reason, Rectitude, and Justice—appearing to her as she sat in her study, depressed after reading yet another book by a man attacking women. They help her build a city, made of the stories of virtuous, wise, and brave women from history, myth, and the Bible, as a refuge and a defense for all women. Christine's book is often regarded as an early work of feminist argument: she insisted that women's apparent inferiority was the result of their lack of education, not their nature. "If it were customary to send little girls to school and teach them the same subjects as are taught to boys," Reason tells her, "they would learn just as fully and would understand the subtleties of all arts and sciences."

## Women in power

Some women wielded great power. **Isabella d'Este** (1474–1539), marchioness of Mantua, was one of the most important patrons of the Renaissance, who collected antiquities and commissioned paintings from Mantegna, Perugino, and others for her private study, and governed Mantua in her husband's absence and after his death. **Caterina Sforza** (1463–1509), countess of Forlì, defended her fortress against the forces of Cesare Borgia in 1499–1500. A famous story, told by Machiavelli among others, claims that years earlier, when rebels who had murdered her husband threatened to kill her children, she stood on the walls and declared that she could make more. Outside Italy, the sixteenth century saw a remarkable number of women rulers: **Isabella of Castile**, **Mary I** and **Elizabeth I** of England, **Mary, Queen of Scots**, and **Catherine de' Medici**, regent of France. Their rule provoked fierce debate about whether women could legitimately govern.

## Women artists

A few women became professional artists, usually as the daughters of painters, trained in their fathers' workshops. **Sofonisba Anguissola** (c. 1532–1625) of Cremona, one of six daughters of a nobleman who gave them all a humanist education, became a court painter to King Philip II of Spain; Vasari visited her and praised her work, and Michelangelo is said to have sent her drawings to copy. In the next century, **Artemisia Gentileschi** (1593–c. 1656), raped as a young woman by her teacher and subjected to torture during the trial that followed, became one of the most celebrated painters of her age, famous for her powerful paintings of biblical heroines such as Judith beheading Holofernes.

## A revised answer

Historians today would give Kelly's question a more complicated answer. The Renaissance did not liberate women, and in some ways it reinforced their subordination. But it also produced, for the first time, a sustained debate about the nature and abilities of women—the *querelle des femmes*, or "quarrel about women," that Christine de Pizan began—and a handful of women who seized the tools of humanism to argue their own case.

> **Pause and consider.** Christine de Pizan argued that women's apparent inferiority came from lack of education. How might a Renaissance humanist have answered her? How might he have agreed?`,
